---
import Exhibit from "../components/Exhibit.astro";
import BreachStrip from "../components/BreachStrip.astro";
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import PractitionerNotes from "../components/PractitionerNotes.astro";
import Definitions from "../components/Definitions.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import { load, pct, type Frame } from "../lib/data";

const meta = load("meta");
const golden = load("golden");
const metrics = load("metrics");
const varAll = load<Record<string, Frame>>("var");
const v99 = metrics.var["0.01"];
const es = metrics.es;
const testDays: number = meta.splits.test.rows;
const rows = [
  { key: "gaussian", name: "Normal distribution (RiskMetrics)" },
  { key: "hist", name: "Historical simulation" },
  { key: "garch", name: "GARCH(1,1)" },
  { key: "fhs", name: "Filtered historical simulation" },
  { key: "aci", name: "Conformal, ACI", focus: true },
].map((r) => ({ ...r, rate: v99[`${r.key}_var`].breach_rate as number }));
const nDays: number = v99.aci_var.days;
const label = `Days each model's 99% one-day loss limit was breached. Normal distribution: ${pct(v99.gaussian_var.breach_rate, 2)} of days; conformal ACI: ${pct(v99.aci_var.breach_rate, 2)}.`;
const esNames: Record<string, string> = {
  gaussian: "Normal distribution", hist: "Historical simulation", garch: "GARCH(1,1)",
  gjr: "GJR-GARCH", fhs: "Filtered historical simulation", split: "Conformal, split",
  rolling: "Conformal, rolling", dtaci: "Conformal, DtACI", aci: "Conformal, ACI",
};
---
<section class="chapter" id="tails" aria-labelledby="tails-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="6" title="Tail risk" minutes={3} question="What is the worst day you should plan for?" hid="tails-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Volatility describes a normal week. Risk managers care most about the bad days, so the same
      machinery is pointed at the left tail of next-day returns. Value at Risk at 99% is the loss the
      portfolio should beat on 99 days out of 100. Over {testDays.toLocaleString("en-GB")} test days, an honest
      model should be breached about {Math.round(testDays * 0.01)} times, and the breaches should be
      scattered, not bunched.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit-wide">
    <Exhibit
      letter="G"
      title="Every day each model's 99% loss limit was breached"
      source={`One-day 99% VaR, ${meta.splits.test.start.slice(0, 4)}–${meta.splits.test.end.slice(0, 4)}. Each red tick is one day the next-day loss was worse than the limit; the number at the right counts them. An honest model has about ${Math.round(testDays * 0.01)}, spread out.`}
      id="exhibit-g"
    >
      <BreachStrip frame={varAll["0.01"]} rows={rows} label={label} />
      <BreachStrip frame={varAll["0.01"]} rows={rows} label={label} narrow />
      <table slot="table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Breaches</th><th scope="col">Breach rate</th><th scope="col">Kupiec p</th><th scope="col">Christoffersen p</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.name}</td>
              <td>{Math.round(r.rate * nDays)}</td>
              <td>{pct(v99[`${r.key}_var`].breach_rate, 2)}</td>
              <td>{v99[`${r.key}_var`].kupiec_p.toFixed(3)}</td>
              <td>{v99[`${r.key}_var`].christoffersen_p.toFixed(3)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Exhibit>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The normal-distribution model's breaches pile up in 2020 and again in later sell-offs: it was
      calm when it should have been wary. The counts hide one thing: the adaptive model's breaches
      also bunch up (its independence test p-value is {v99.aci_var.christoffersen_p.toFixed(3)}), so it
      passes the rate test but fails the clustering test. Split and rolling conformal pass both, and so
      does filtered historical simulation, a strong classical model. Chapter 10 comes back to that.
    </p>
    <p>
      VaR says where the bad days start, not how bad they get. Regulators now ask for <Term
        id="es"
        title="Expected Shortfall: the average loss on the days worse than the VaR level.">Expected Shortfall</Term
      > at 97.5%: the average loss on the worst 2.5% of days. Conformal methods give it directly, as the
      average of the past misses beyond the cut-off.
    </p>
  </div>

  <table class="data">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Expected Shortfall backtest at 97.5%</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col" class="num">Days past 97.5% VaR</th><th scope="col" class="num">Average ES</th><th scope="col" class="num">Z₂ score</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {Object.entries(esNames).map(([k, name]) => (
        <tr class={k === "aci" ? "focus" : ""}>
          <th scope="row">{name}</th>
          <td class="num">{pct(es[k].breach_rate, 2)}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(es[k].mean_es, 2)}</td>
          <td class="num">{es[k].z2.toFixed(2)}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="table-note">
    Target 2.50% of days. Z₂ (Acerbi and Székely) near 0 means the average shortfall was about right;
    below −0.70 would mean it was understated. Every model stays in the green zone; the adaptive conformal
    ones sit closest to 0.
  </p>

  <PractitionerNotes>
    <p>
      Conformal VaR uses a one-sided lower-tail score on next-day returns with the ACI update and an α
      floor of {golden.floor_ratio}α, which keeps the limit finite through breach clusters at 99%. Kupiec is the
      unconditional coverage likelihood-ratio test; Christoffersen tests independence of breaches.
      ES for the conformal methods is the mean of calibration scores beyond the quantile; Z₂ uses the
      realized loss on breach days scaled by the forecast ES.
    </p>
  </PractitionerNotes>

  <Definitions items={[{ id: "es", term: "Expected Shortfall", def: "The average loss on the days worse than the VaR level; Basel's preferred tail measure." }]} />
  <Pager id="tails" />
</section>
